{"candidates":[{"claim":"A symmetry-pruned randomized search starting from Batcher's odd-even mergesort network (size 48) is more likely to find a 45-comparator network under a fixed budget than a naive random removal of comparators, because reflection symmetry i -> n-1-i reduces the search space size by a factor of 2 without losing optimality.","counterconditions":["Strategy 1 finds a valid 45-comparator network before Strategy 2 under the same budget","Neither strategy finds a valid 45-comparator network within the fixed budget","Reflection symmetry does not map valid sorting networks to valid sorting networks of equal size"],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"bfc42b3064ed02fe","quote":"- Symmetry: the reflection i -> n-1-i maps sorting networks to sorting networks of equal size, so search need only consider one member of each symmetry class. -"}],"mechanism":"Simulate a search where we start with Batcher's 48-comparator network. Strategy 1: randomly remove 3 comparators and test if the resulting 45-comparator network sorts all 8192 binary vectors. Strategy 2: randomly remove pairs of reflection-symmetric comparators (reducing by 2) and a single comparator, testing if the 45-comparator network sorts. Under a fixed budget of 10,000 trials, Strategy 2 should yield more valid sorting networks than Strategy 1, discriminating whether symmetry pruning provides a computational advantage in finding structural improvements to sorting networks.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-c684127f981a8a8c1d5e0739c804e68436eebc3d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7}],"simulation_proposals":[]}